** System Design Decision Tree: AoS vs SoA + Event vs Tick
*** 1. Start: Write pseudocode (NO data layout decisions)
   - Define gameplay logic in abstract form
   - Identify:
     - what changes
     - when it changes
     - how often it runs
   - DO NOT think about structs or arrays yet
   - pass only domains (XDatabase&) as parameters
     - specify after parameter list what references are accessed

   /Goal: describe behavior, not storage/
*** 2. Classify each system by access pattern
**** 2.1 Entity-centric logic (many fields per entity)
    - Example:
      - AI decision making
      - inventory logic
      - combat resolution
    - Pattern:
      - one entity → many fields accessed together
    - Candidate: AoS / chunked AoS

**** 2.2 Field-centric logic (one field across many entities)
    - Example:
      - hunger decay
      - physics integration
      - animation updates
    - Pattern:
      - one operation → many entities
    - Candidate: SoA

**** 2.3 Reactive / sparse logic (only some entities update)
    - Example:
      - starvation triggers
      - job assignment
      - events / state transitions
    - Pattern:
      - only "active" entities matter
    - Candidate: event queue / active set

*** 3. Classify execution model (independent axis)
**** 3.1 Tick-based systems
    - runs every frame or fixed interval
    - good for:
      - continuous simulation (hunger, physics)
    - typically SoA-friendly

**** 3.2 Event-driven systems
    - runs only when triggered
    - good for:
      - thresholds
      - state changes
      - rare interactions
    - typically AoS-friendly or hybrid

**** 3.3 Hybrid systems
    - tick updates state
    - events react to thresholds
    - most real game systems fall here

*** 4. Choose data layout AFTER classification
**** 4.1 AoS / chunked AoS
    - use when:
      - multiple fields accessed per entity
      - entity-centric logic dominates
      - sparse updates
    - benefits:
      - cache-friendly per entity
      - simple reasoning

**** 4.2 SoA
    - use when:
      - one field updated across many entities
      - SIMD/vectorization possible
      - bulk processing dominates
    - benefits:
      - memory bandwidth efficient
      - fast streaming

**** 4.3 Hybrid / ECS-style
    - use when:
      - mixed workloads
      - multiple systems with different patterns
    - typical engine solution

*** 5. Implement hot systems first
   - identify performance-critical loops
   - ensure:
     - correct memory access pattern
     - minimal indirection
     - contiguous iteration where possible

*** 6. Implement event-driven systems second
   - use:
     - queues (push-based)
     - scheduled events
     - active entity sets
   - avoid:
     - full scans over all entities
     - flag-based O(N) sweeps as primary mechanism

*** 7. Validate with profiling
   - check:
     - cache misses
     - memory bandwidth usage
     - branch mispredictions
   - adjust layout ONLY based on measured bottlenecks

*** 8. Final optimization phase
   - split SoA where bulk data dominates
   - merge into AoS where per-entity locality dominates
   - introduce chunking if both are needed
   - avoid premature global redesigns
